#use(std/code)
#use(std/math: asciimath)

#section{Loading Plugins (##use)}::

#code{#use(mod, …)} imports a plugin — a host module exporting functions and,
optionally, body readers — into the document. It is a top-level reader
directive: the module's body readers apply for the rest of the file

Module path mapping:

#list
	#item: #code{std/x} → the bundled #code{yamd/std/x} (e.g. #code{#use(std/table)})
	#item
		#code{mod: variant} → #code{mod/variant} — a variant #emph{binding}: #code{#use(std/math: asciimath)} loads
		#code{yamd/std/math/asciimath}, choosing the math notation. The core knows nothing about math
	#item: #code{"./file.rkt"} — a string is a file path relative to the document, for project-local plugins
	#item: Any other symbol is a collection path, verbatim

Several modules may be listed in one #code{#use}, comma- or
whitespace-separated

#code(lang: "plain")
	#use(std/math: asciimath)
	Inline math now works: #m{sum_(i=0)^n i^2}.

Live (this page's top #code{#use} bound the asciimath variant):

Inline math now works: #m{sum_(i=0)^n i^2}

#section{Plugin Search Path}::

Collection paths resolve through Racket's collection search, so
#code{PLTCOLLECTS} is the #code{PYTHONPATH} equivalent:

#code(lang: "plain")
	PLTCOLLECTS=":/path/to/plugins" yamd doc.yamd

makes #code{#use(mycol/mod)} find #code{/path/to/plugins/mycol/mod.rkt}. The
leading colon keeps Racket's default collection paths in the search list —
omit it and the defaults are dropped. The packaged #code{yamd} binary
(#code{nix run}) appends its own collection at runtime, so your
#code{PLTCOLLECTS} is respected. For a durable mapping prefer
#code{raco pkg install --link mycol/}; for project-local plugins the
document-relative string form needs no search path at all

Corner cases:

#list
	#item
		#code{#use} merges the plugin's body readers at #emph{read} time — put it before the first use of the plugin's
		forms, conventionally at the top of the file
	#item: #code{#use} is not available inside host code; the reader rejects it there
	#item
		In a doc-set, each file's #code{#use} affects only that file (children of #code{#include}/#code{#toctree} evaluate
		in fresh namespaces), but a plugin is instantiated once per process, so plugin module-level state is shared
	#item
		#code{std/base} (#code{emph}, #code{strong}, #code{section}, …) is loaded by default; everything else needs an
		explicit #code{#use}
